WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:02.919
Service Analytics measures the percentage of

00:02.932 --> 00:05.432
completed deliveries that arrived on time.

00:05.816 --> 00:08.622
It excludes parcels still awaiting delivery.

00:09.030 --> 00:13.953
At one in the afternoon, its Delivery performance report shows 100%.

00:14.362 --> 00:15.892
What produced that number?

00:16.300 --> 00:19.846
This is an invented incident, checked using SQLite.

00:20.872 --> 00:23.525
A scheduled job calculates the percentage from

00:23.537 --> 00:26.063
the delivery records and saves the result.

00:26.472 --> 00:30.197
The report reads that saved result when someone refreshes it.

00:30.592 --> 00:33.896
Refreshing the report does not rerun the calculation.

00:34.304 --> 00:37.837
Those are separate steps, so we need evidence connecting them.

00:38.872 --> 00:42.596
Lineage records where data comes from, how it is transformed,

00:42.864 --> 00:45.441
and which datasets or reports depend on it.

00:45.824 --> 00:50.964
Provenance records the history of a particular dataset or result: who or what created

00:50.977 --> 00:54.459
or changed it, when, and using which sources and methods.

00:54.868 --> 00:59.459
The terms overlap, but a route alone does not explain this particular number.

01:00.497 --> 01:04.234
Start with the report refresh record, F1300.

01:04.643 --> 01:10.064
It says the report refreshed at one in the afternoon and read output summary-R1205.

01:10.433 --> 01:13.405
That output identifier is the link to follow.

01:13.814 --> 01:17.692
The refresh time alone does not tell us when the result was produced.

01:18.705 --> 01:22.302
The matching published output contains 100%.

01:22.711 --> 01:27.686
It was published at twelve oh five, fifty-five minutes before the report refresh.

01:28.055 --> 01:30.912
Its run identifier is R1205.

01:31.321 --> 01:36.270
We have connected the report to a producing execution, but the stored percentage does

01:36.283 --> 01:38.974
not establish whether the calculation was correct.

01:39.997 --> 01:42.497
The run record supplies two more references:

01:42.816 --> 01:47.305
input snapshot S1205, and code version completed-v1.

01:47.715 --> 01:52.153
A snapshot is a retained copy of the input data at a particular time.

01:52.549 --> 01:56.885
These references tell us which saved input and calculation to inspect.

01:57.255 --> 01:59.768
Today’s table and code may be different.

02:00.788 --> 02:03.850
The saved input was captured at twelve oh five.

02:04.220 --> 02:06.733
All four parcels were promised by noon.

02:07.117 --> 02:08.749
Two had arrived on time.

02:09.118 --> 02:10.789
Two had not yet arrived.

02:11.173 --> 02:15.420
In this complete example, an empty delivery time means unfinished.

02:15.828 --> 02:20.510
These saved rows are input data; the run’s reference to them is metadata.

02:21.538 --> 02:25.148
The retained calculation excludes unfinished deliveries.

02:25.532 --> 02:29.435
Only the two completed parcels remain, and both were on time.

02:29.804 --> 02:33.630
Two on time out of two completed gives 100%.

02:34.039 --> 02:38.121
The saved input and code explain why this run produced that result.

02:39.163 --> 02:44.380
Follow the report refresh to its output, the output to its producing run, and the run

02:44.393 --> 02:46.230
to its retained input and code.

02:46.601 --> 02:49.458
Matching identifiers connect the evidence.

02:49.866 --> 02:53.271
We now know why the saved result was 100%.

02:53.680 --> 02:57.493
Whether it was still current when the report refreshed is a separate question,

02:57.761 --> 02:59.241
explored in Chapter 4.
